<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vaishnavism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vaishnavism is one of the main traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, centred on the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and his avatars, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, as the supreme god. It developed from devotional cults of the late 1st millennium BCE and flourished under many Indian dynasties, inspiring the bhakti movement and teachers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhva</a> and Chaitanya. Its followers are called Vaishnavas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vaishnavism.jpg" alt="Vaishnavism" width="320" height="597" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unnamed photographer, image listed as public domain on the source page of Los Angeles Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindu_God_Vishnu_as_Narayana_witth_Consorts_10_AD_Sculpture_at_LA_County_Museum_of_Art.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kirtivarman’s brother Mangalesha (r. c. 597–610) defeated the Kalachuris of Mahishmati and took Revatidvipa, near modern Goa, and an inscription of 578 in the <b>Vaishnava</b> cave at Badami records that he had it excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to tradition Vishnuvardhana himself turned to <b>Vaishnavism</b> under the influence of Ramanuja, the Sri Vaishnava philosopher who lived at Melkote in the Hoysala country, while Shaivism flourished too, and the Lingayat movement spread among the population in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was organised around temples, Brahmin assemblies, agricultural villages and merchant communities, and the kings lavished wealth on the great temples of Madurai, Srirangam and Chidambaram, where Saiva and <b>Vaishnava</b> devotion was central to public life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Pallava patronage Kanchipuram, counted among the seven sacred cities of Hinduism, flourished as a centre of Sanskrit learning, and the bhakti hymns of the Nayanars and Alvars composed in their age became the scriptures of Tamil Shaivism and <b>Vaishnavism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Shaiva sects, including the Kalamukha ascetics who ran large temple colleges at Balligave and elsewhere, enjoyed strong royal patronage; Jainism remained influential, especially among merchants and noblewomen such as Attimabbe, who sponsored copies of Ponna’s works and a Jain temple at Lakkundi; and <b>Vaishnavism</b> grew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hinduism, in the forms of <b>Vaishnavism</b>, Kaumaram, Shaktism, Shaivism and Dravidian folk religion, together with Jainism and Buddhism, was present in the Tamil country from at least the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
